  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 345 AM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nUnfortunately, an upper trough will begin developing over West Texas \nand Southeast New Mexico late Wednesday afternoon, and persist \nThursday and Friday.  This will shave 2-3 F off of highs Thursday \nand Friday, and result in low chances of showers and thunderstorms \neach afternoon south of I-10.  For those who prefer warmer \ntemperatures, a deep trough will begin developing over the west \ncoast over the weekend, shoving the upper trough to the east and \nonce again increasing thicknesses.  Temperatures will then warm \nthrough the weekend, topping out Columbus Day some 8-10 F above \nnormal, with 90s expected in the river valleys.  By this time, West \nTexas and Southeast New Mexico will be under progressive southwest \nflow aloft, and shortwaves moving through this flow could kick off a \nfew showers and thunderstorms Monday afternoon.  Lamentably, QPF \nremains tepid.\n\nJust beyond the extended, the GFS once again increases PWATs at KMAF \nto 1.92\" by 12Z Tuesday, saturating the column to 300 mb. \nClimatology puts the 100th percentile at 1.45\".  Even the ECMWF \ncomes in at 1.63\".  All this is in response to the west coast trough \nbeginning to eject to the Four Corners.  This has a good chance of \ndisappearing over the next couple of runs, but we'll keep an eye on \nit. Stay tuned...",
